meta {
  name: Register this device for push notifications (POST /v1/notifications/register)
  type: http
  seq: 135
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/register
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{sessionToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "deviceUuid": "8B2F1C4E-6A3D-4F19-9C77-1E5A0D2B7F33",
    "notificationPublicKey": "did:key:zDnaeoYytsARBq9NiBk1TbJESQcPRy5RPVvdT7FqpjrQtJ5DL",
    "apnsToken": "740f4707bebcf74f9b7c25d48e3358945f6aa01da5ddb387462c7eaf61bb78ad",
    "apnsTopic": "org.obsign.identitywallet",
    "ping": false
  }
}

vars:pre-request {
  baseUrl: http://localhost:8080
}

docs {
  # POST /v1/notifications/register

  Register this device to receive end-to-end-encrypted push notifications. Authenticate with
  the account's access token (Bearer, or DPoP for an OAuth-bound token).

  Custos cannot push to Apple itself — APNs auth keys are bundle-id-bound, so a self-hosted
  instance structurally cannot. Instead it hands HPKE-sealed payloads to a **blind courier**
  relay along with an opaque per-device handle. The relay never sees plaintext, a DID, a
  handle, or your APNs token in a form it can attribute to you.

  ## Request

  - `deviceUuid` — an app-generated stable identifier for this device. Deliberately *not* the
    server's `devices.id`: those rows are deleted when the account's DID is promoted, so a
    registration anchored there would die exactly when the account starts mattering.
  - `notificationPublicKey` — the device's P-256 `did:key`. Payloads are sealed to it, so only
    this device can open them. Generated on-device; the private half never leaves it.
  - `apnsToken` — the APNs device token (hex), from `didRegisterForRemoteNotifications`.
  - `apnsTopic` — the app's bundle id.
  - `ping` (optional, default `false`) — metadata-minimizing ping mode. When `true`, this
    device receives a content-free `content-available: 1` background push instead of a sealed
    payload: no `ezpds` envelope reaches the relay or Apple at all — they see only that
    *something* happened — and the app fetches the actual events from its Custos on wake.
    The trade-off is delivery quality: iOS aggressively throttles background pushes.

  Re-registering the same `deviceUuid` replaces the row, so call this on every APNs token
  change without accumulating stale registrations.

  ## Response (200)

  ```json
  { "status": "pending" }
  ```

  Always `"pending"`: the row is stored and the relay handle is minted in the background.
  Registration deliberately does not wait on the relay — this is one of the first calls the app
  makes after promotion, and a push relay's uptime does not belong in the onboarding critical
  path for a feature whose whole failure mode is a missed banner.

  ## Errors

  - `400` — bad `deviceUuid`, a `notificationPublicKey` that is not a P-256 `did:key`, a
    non-hex `apnsToken`, or an `apnsTopic` that is not a bundle identifier
  - `401` — missing/invalid access token
  - `501` — this instance has no `[notifications] relay` configured (push is unavailable here)
}
